{
  "turn": 1,
  "input": "Iris, while we sit together here, how do you usually speak when something is difficult to say? Do you soften the words or speak plainly? Describe your usual habit in your own words.",
  "first_physical": 1,
  "last_physical": 2,
  "outcome": {
    "ok": true,
    "turn_id": "d09_unique_value:r2:t1",
    "narration": "Iris was quiet for a moment. She sat on one of the simple stools near the patient beds, hands resting in her lap, the sparse floor stretching out around them — two bed frames, two wardrobes, the empty cabinets across the way, and the spiral stair curving down along the perimeter wall.\n\n\"I don't know that I have a usual,\" she said. Her voice carried no particular affectation — neither especially gentle nor especially hard. \"I haven't had enough difficult conversations to have settled on a habit.\" She turned a hand over, looked at her own knuckles, then set it back down. \"When something is hard, I expect I just say it and then wish I hadn't, or don't say it and wish I had. That's the honest answer.\"",
